<!-- Phase 5 website teaching layer; original chapter remains read-only. Evidence: website/reviews/android-phase5-findings.md. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A restored app finds encrypted files but cannot open its vault. The ciphertext arrived; the device-bound key did not. Start with custody and recovery: what stays on the device, what the app can use, and what the user can recover.</p>

<!-- @after Application Decisions :: animation -->
### Key custody versus stored data

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Restore is a recovery path, not a key transfer</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node" data-el="key" data-art="key" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Keystore key</strong><small data-text="keyV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="data" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>App-private ciphertext</strong><small data-text="dataV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="session" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Product session</strong><small data-text="sessionV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{
"label": "Original device",
"caption": "The app uses a key handle to operate on its stored ciphertext.",
"state": {
"key": "active",
"data": "done"
},
"text": {
"keyV": "handle available",
"dataV": "encrypted record",
"sessionV": "signed in"
},
"add": [
"key",
"data",
"session"
]
},
{
"label": "Restore elsewhere",
"caption": "Suppose ciphertext is restored without its original device-bound key.",
"state": {
"key": "ghost",
"data": "active"
},
"text": {
"keyV": "not available here",
"dataV": "bytes restored"
}
},
{
"label": "Recover",
"caption": "Apply the vault’s recovery policy and establish a new session; never treat undecryptable data as plaintext.",
"state": {
"session": "active"
},
"text": {
"sessionV": "re-authenticate or designed recovery"
}
}
]</script><figcaption>Key availability and ciphertext availability are separate facts.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Review Checklist :: check -->
### Try the decision

<details><summary>Non-exportable means hardware-backed?</summary>

No. Non-exportable means the app never receives the key material; hardware backing (TEE or StrongBox) is a separate property you must request and verify.
</details>

<details><summary>Key invalidated: loop on decrypt?</summary>

No. KeyPermanentlyInvalidatedException is permanent: that key will never decrypt again. Delete the key and the ciphertext that depends on it, then re-enroll or re-authenticate, as the chapter's Failure Modes table describes.
</details>

<details><summary>Does encryption replace server authorization?</summary>

No. Local encryption protects data at rest on the device; the server still decides who may read or change each resource.
</details>

<!-- @after Failure Modes :: animation -->
### When a key stops working for good

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">From enrollment change to a clean re-enrollment</span><div class="stage viz-grid" data-cols="4"><div class="node" data-el="change" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>Device change</strong><small>biometric or lock-screen enrollment</small></div><div class="node" data-el="key" data-art="key" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Keystore key</strong><small data-text="keyV">usable</small></div><div class="node" data-el="data" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Ciphertext</strong><small data-text="dataV">encrypted with that key</small></div><div class="node is-key" data-el="user" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>User flow</strong><small data-text="userV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"Change","caption":"The user changes biometric or lock-screen enrollment. For a key bound to that authentication state, the platform invalidates the key.","add":["change","key","data","user"],"state":{"change":"active"}},
{"label":"Decrypt fails","caption":"The next use fails with KeyPermanentlyInvalidatedException. Permanently means what it says: retrying will never make this key decrypt again.","state":{"change":"done","key":"error"},"text":{"keyV":"permanently invalidated"}},
{"label":"Clean up","caption":"Delete the key and the ciphertext that depends on it, as the chapter's Failure Modes table says. Keeping unreadable data only produces later failures.","state":{"data":"warn"},"text":{"dataV":"deleted with the key"}},
{"label":"Re-establish","caption":"Re-authenticate the user, create a new key, and fetch or re-create the protected data from its owner, often the server.","state":{"key":"done","data":"done","user":"done"},"text":{"keyV":"new key","dataV":"re-created","userV":"signed in again, no retry loop"}}
]</script><figcaption>Use Previous, Next or Play; every step is also readable as text.</figcaption></figure>
